  "notes": "Philadelphia spoken-word-to-soprano soul over a working band. The band is the catalogue spine the research folds out — 62–102 straight with the backbeat on 2&4 — and the keys are the named ones, D♭ minor/D♭ major and G major with F♯ minor and B♭ minor for the slow material. The swing is pulled below the parent because the entry says \"a lightly swung 16th feel\" where the genre model is heavy. Drums are a live kit with \"snare late, ride rather than hats on the slow material, and a full hand-percussion layer\", which is the offGrid window, the ride lane and the 8th/16th split on the hat base rather than a flat 16th carpet. Harmony carries no roman numerals in the entry — it names \"jazz-club: m9, 13ths, tritone substitutions on turnarounds\" and \"4-to-8-bar cycles with functional cadences and modulating bridges\" — so no families are authored and the delta is the extension spread, the open voicing and a harmonic rhythm that keeps real motion. The melody follows the vocal-type claim: she is \"a soprano who draws on her upper register\" and \"puts bass in her soprano voice\", so the register is the widest in this batch, and the load-bearing habit — \"spoken word inside the song form\", verses that drop out of pitch into recitative — is encoded as a raised `emptyBarBias` and a question-answer structure rather than as pitch content. Bass is the named walking, chromatic-approach player with \"real countermelodic content in the gaps between vocal phrases\", hence the low root probability and the wide passing-tone set. ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are all drum probabilities and velocities, `swing.amount` 0.58 (the research says \"lightly swung\", not a ratio), and every melody and countermelody number. ⚠ `confidence` is medium: the research rates the entry high-medium and says per-track producer attributions are thin.",
